10) REAPER philosophy doc (concise, inspired, pragmatic)
REAPER — language philosophy (one pager)
REAPER exists to collapse a full trading execution stack into a single, efficient skull. It is opinionated: it favors determinism, explicitness, and performance over general-purpose convenience.

Core tenets:

Intent-first syntax
Users express what they want (find arb, execute, flash), not how the machine should implement it. The compiler and runtime convert intent into low-level optimized flows.

Safe defaults, escape hatches for pros
REAPER enforces safe memory and sandbox defaults for user scripts, but provides unsafe/@no_heap/@hot annotations for power users to cross boundaries when needed.

Predictability over magic
Avoid runtime surprises: no hidden allocations in hot code, no implicit retries, and a small, well-documented set of builtins. When magic exists (intrinsics), it's explicit and auditable.

Vertical integration
Language, compiler, runtime, turbo-RPC, and mempool ingestion are designed together. Vertical integration unlocks optimizations impossible across generic toolchains.

Human-first
REAPER’s surface must be readable, debuggable, and teachable. Humans write strategies — machines execute them faster than anyone else. Keep the language simple; make the backend do the heavy lifting.

Instrument everything
Every execution path is observable. Trade decisions are logged with structured metadata to allow audits, replays, and ML training.

Fail-safe as default
Misconfigured scripts should not drain keys. Signing requires explicit permission; test and staging modes are first-class citizens.

Composability
Small explicit building blocks compose into complex strategies. Encourage small functions and discourage monolithic scripts that are hard to verify.

